---

<BaseLayout
  title={svc.titleShort}
  description={svc.metaDescription}
  path="/services/bed-bug-treatment/"
  altLangPath="/es/servicios/tratamiento-de-chinches/"
  quickAnswer={quickAnswer}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  faqs={faqs}
  service={{
    name: svc.name,
    description: 'Discreet chemical bed bug treatment with scheduled follow-up for homes, rental property and lodging across Gaines County and the Permian Basin.',
  }}
>
  <Crumbs items={crumbs} />

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">{svc.licenceCategory} · licensed category</p>
      <h1>Bed Bug Treatment</h1>
      <div class="measure"><AnswerBox text={quickAnswer} /></div>
      <Trust />
      <p class="measure" style="margin-top:1rem"><a href="/es/servicios/tratamiento-de-chinches/" lang="es" hreflang="es">Esta página en español</a></p>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Start with the thing that stops most people picking up the phone for weeks:
          <strong>bed bugs have nothing to do with how clean your house is.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          They are an introduction problem. They arrive in luggage, in second-hand furniture, in a bag
          set down in the wrong place, on clothing, in a moving box. Immaculate homes get them.
          Hospitals get them. Expensive hotels get them. The only thing housekeeping affects is how hard
          they are to find, because clutter gives them more places to hide.
        </p>
        <p>
          The delay caused by embarrassment is the actual damage. A small, localised introduction is a
          contained job. The same introduction eight weeks later has spread through a room and into
          adjacent ones, and it is a much bigger, longer and more expensive piece of work. Calling early
          is the single most effective thing you can do, and nobody here is going to make you feel bad
          about it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What we do not offer, said first</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Most pages about bed bugs list capabilities. This one starts with the limits, because in this
          particular trade the limits are what you need in order to choose properly.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>We do not do heat treatment.</strong> Whole-room or whole-structure thermal remediation
          requires specific equipment and specific training, and we do not carry it. It is a real method
          with real advantages — it reaches harbourage chemicals struggle with, and it has no residual
          consideration — and where it is the right answer for a property, you need somebody who does it.
          We will tell you that on the phone.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>We do not fumigate.</strong> Structural fumigation requires its own certification, which
          is not held here.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>We do not promise a single-visit resolution.</strong> It is not achievable honestly with
          conventional treatment, and the follow-up section below explains why.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And we do not sell a bed bug scare.</strong> Bed bugs are a genuine misery — sleep loss,
          stress, and a real financial cost — and they are not a health emergency in the way this industry
          sometimes implies. You have time to make a considered decision, get a second quote and choose the
          right method. Anyone pressuring you to sign today is selling, not diagnosing.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What we do offer</strong> is conventional chemical treatment, performed by the licence
          holder personally, with a scheduled follow-up, a written scope agreed before the work, and
          discretion.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Finding out whether you actually have them</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          A surprising share of bed bug calls turn out to be something else, and a surprising share of
          genuine infestations go unrecognised for months. Both errors come from looking at the wrong
          evidence.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Bites are not diagnostic.</strong> People react enormously differently — some develop
          pronounced welts, some show nothing at all, and two people in the same bed can have completely
          different experiences. Several other things bite. Reading an infestation from skin is guesswork.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The physical evidence is what counts.</strong> Small dark spots — digested blood — along
          mattress seams, on the box spring, on the underside of the frame, and at the joints where a bed
          comes apart. Shed skins, which look like translucent empty shells. Live insects roughly the size
          and shape of an apple seed, flattened, reddish-brown, and faster than people expect.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Where to look, in order.</strong> Mattress seams and tags first. Then the box spring,
          including underneath the fabric at the corners. Then the frame, especially anywhere two pieces
          bolt together. Then the headboard, and behind it if it is mounted to the wall. Then within about
          fifteen feet of where somebody sleeps: nightstands, the seam of an upholstered chair, behind a
          picture frame, along the edge of carpet, in the joint of a baseboard.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Photograph what you find.</strong> Phone camera, as close as it will focus, with
          something for scale. Text it. That resolves in ten seconds what five minutes of description
          cannot, and it means the visit arrives already knowing what it is dealing with.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And if you are not sure, call anyway.</strong> An inspection that finds nothing is a good
          outcome, not a wasted trip. It is also considerably cheaper than the alternative of waiting to be
          certain.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Why one visit is not the job</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          This is the part of bed bug work most misunderstood by customers and most often glossed over by
          companies, and it explains almost every disappointing outcome in this trade.
        </p>
        <p>
          A conventional treatment deals effectively with the insects present at the time it is applied.
          It does considerably less to eggs. Eggs then hatch on their own schedule, producing a new
          generation from a population you were told had been dealt with — which arrives days or weeks
          later and feels exactly like a treatment failure.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is not a failure. It is the reason the second visit exists, and the reason the interval
          between visits is set by the biology rather than by convenience. Skipping it, or letting it slip
          because the return drive is inconvenient, is how a job that was going to work stops working.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Which is why an insect growth regulator is part of the treatment</strong> rather than an
          extra. Precor IGR addresses the part of the population that is not currently a reproducing
          adult, which is the part a straight adulticide misses.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And it is why seeing an insect after the first visit is expected</strong> rather than
          alarming. What matters is the trend across visits, not the presence or absence of a sighting on
          day four. We will tell you what to expect and roughly when, so that you are measuring the right
          thing.
        </p>
        <p>
          The practical implication for choosing a company: ask what the follow-up schedule is, in visits
          and in days, before you book. If the answer is vague, or if follow-up is priced as a separate
          call-out, that tells you how the job is actually going to run.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Preparation, and why it is not busywork</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Bed bug treatment asks more of the customer than any other service on this site, and it is
          reasonable to want to know why before doing it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The reason is access. Bed bugs live in seams, joints and crevices within a short distance of
          where people sleep, and treatment has to reach those places. Every item in the way is a place
          that does not get treated and a place the population can retreat to.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Laundry.</strong> Bedding, and clothing from affected rooms, washed and dried on high
          heat. The dryer does the work rather than the wash. Bagged after, and kept separate until the
          job is complete.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Clutter reduction, not deep cleaning.</strong> The goal is access, not spotlessness.
          Clearing the floor around beds and furniture, emptying nightstands, and moving stored boxes out
          of the treatment zone — into sealed bags rather than into another room, because moving an
          unsealed box is how a one-room problem becomes a two-room problem.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Do not throw the mattress out yet.</strong> The instinct is strong and it is usually
          wrong. A treated mattress with an encasement fitted is generally recoverable, and a discarded
          mattress dragged through the house on its way to the kerb spreads the problem along the route.
          If it does need to go, it goes after treatment and wrapped.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Do not start moving beds into other rooms.</strong> Or sleeping on the sofa. Both are
          understandable and both spread the infestation, because the insects follow where people sleep.
          Staying put until the work is done is genuinely the better strategy.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Do not spray retail product first.</strong> Many retail products are repellent, which
          scatters a contained population into new harbourage rather than eliminating it, and it makes the
          job harder and longer. If you already have, say so — it changes the plan and it is information,
          not a confession.
        </p>
        <p>
          A specific preparation list is provided before the visit, matched to your property. It will be
          shorter than the ones circulating online, because it is aimed at access rather than at making the
          customer responsible for the result.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Rental property, lodging and the Permian pattern</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Bed bugs behave differently in this region than the national advice assumes, because of how
          people move through it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Short-cycle turnover.</strong> The Permian economy produces rental property that changes
          hands fast — man camps, RV parks, short-term housing, units that fill in a week and empty in a
          month. Each turnover is an introduction opportunity, and the interval between them is short
          enough that a problem can be handed forward repeatedly before anyone identifies it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong><a href="/commercial/hotels/">Lodging</a> on the interstate.</strong> Stanton sits directly on I-20 between Midland and Big
          Spring, and any premises serving a passing population sees a rate of introduction that
          residential property does not. For those operators the game is detection rather than reaction:
          routine inspection of rooms during turnover finds an introduction while it is still one room.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>High-rental towns.</strong> In Lubbock, more than half of occupied housing is rented. In
          Seagraves the figure is around forty percent. Multi-unit and adjacent-unit property raises a
          specific question that single-family housing does not: whether the source is your unit at all.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Who pays, and who authorises.</strong> Texas property law places obligations on
          residential landlords regarding conditions materially affecting health and safety, and many
          leases address pest control directly, sometimes splitting it by pest type or by tenancy length.
          Read the lease before assuming. We are not lawyers and this is not legal advice — but
          practically, we will speak to whoever authorises the work, invoice whoever is paying, and
          coordinate access with whoever lives there, without those needing to be the same person.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The one thing that causes problems</strong> is a tenant booking work the owner has not
          approved and expecting the owner to pay afterwards. Sort authorisation before the visit.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And for multi-unit property:</strong> treating one unit while an adjacent one is
          untreated is frequently money spent against continuous re-supply. Where that is the situation we
          will say so, even though the honest recommendation is a larger job than the one you called about.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What the visit is actually like</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          People ask this more than they ask about method, and it is a fair question, so here is the shape
          of it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The inspection comes first and takes longer than you expect.</strong> Beds are taken
          apart. Mattresses are lifted and the seams and tags examined. Box springs get looked at
          underneath, which means the fabric on the corners. Frames are checked where they bolt together.
          Headboards come off the wall if they are mounted. Then the fifteen-foot radius around each
          sleeping position — nightstands, upholstered furniture seams, carpet edges, baseboard joints,
          behind wall-mounted items.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Then a conversation before anything is applied.</strong> What was found, in which rooms,
          how established it appears to be, what the treatment plan is, what the follow-up schedule is and
          what it will cost. If the inspection changes the scope — and it often does, because infestations
          are rarely confined to the room that prompted the call — the revised number is discussed on the
          spot rather than appearing later on an invoice.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Then treatment</strong>, placed according to the harbourage found rather than applied
          broadly, using Alpine WSG, Precor IGR and Transport as the situation calls for.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Then the paperwork Texas requires.</strong> Under 4 TAC §7.147 the Texas Department of
          Agriculture's own consumer information sheet is made available for an indoor treatment at a
          private residence, in English or Spanish. In rental property with five or more units, at
          workplaces and at hotels and motels, 4 TAC §7.146 adds a pest control sign that we must supply
          to the owner or manager <em>at least forty-eight hours before</em> the treatment, for them to
          post in an area of common access. Keep whatever you are given.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Then the follow-up, on the date agreed.</strong> Not when it is convenient for a route.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Not bringing them home in the first place</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Introduction is the whole mechanism, so prevention is entirely about the things that come into
          the building.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Travel.</strong> Put luggage on the rack rather than the bed or the floor, and pull back
          the sheet at the head of the mattress to look at the seam before you unpack. It takes twenty
          seconds. On returning, unpack into the laundry rather than into the wardrobe, and run everything
          through a hot dryer cycle — the dryer, not the wash, is what does the work.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Second-hand furniture.</strong> The most common domestic introduction route by a wide
          margin, and upholstered items and bed frames are the highest risk. Inspect seams, joints and the
          underside before it comes through the door, not after.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Anything from a kerb.</strong> A mattress or sofa put out on a kerb in this region has a
          meaningful chance of having been put there for a reason.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Encasements.</strong> A mattress and box spring encasement is inexpensive, permanent and
          does two useful things: it removes the most complicated harbourage in the room from the equation,
          and it makes future detection dramatically easier, because anything present has to be on the
          outside where it can be seen.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Reduce the hiding places near the bed.</strong> Not for tidiness — for detection. The
          fewer crevices within fifteen feet of where somebody sleeps, the earlier an introduction is
          noticed, and earlier is the entire difference between a contained job and a whole-home one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Where we do this work</h2>
      <div class="grid c3" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
        {TOWNS.map((t) => (
          <a class="card" href={`/locations/${t.slug}/`}>
            <h3>{t.name}, TX</h3>
            <p>{t.county}{t.distanceMiApprox > 0 ? ` · about ${t.distanceMiApprox} miles ${t.direction} of Seminole` : ' · home market'}</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="measure" style="margin-top:2rem">
        <p>
          Individually quoted after an inspection, because a light localised introduction and a severe
          whole-home infestation are not the same job and should not be priced as though they were. The
          scope, the follow-up schedule and the terms are agreed in writing before any work starts. This
          service carries its own terms rather than the standard 30-day pest guarantee. No heat treatment
          and no fumigation. Discreet. Military discount. Cash, check, card in person or online, Venmo,
          CashApp or Zelle. Se habla español.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Faq items={faqs} heading="Bed bug questions" />
  <Cta
    line="Text a photograph of what you have found if you can — mattress seam, frame joint, or the insect itself. It gets you a straight answer faster than anything else, and nobody here is going to make it awkward."
  />
  <SpokeLinks service="bed-bug-treatment" />

</BaseLayout>
